{"conjecture": "Specifying programs as dependent intersection types—where a value must simultaneously inhabit an implementation type and a proposition type constrained by that implementation's interface—enables fully computational extraction of code that provably holds its commitments without external verification. In this scheme, the refinement type itself computes the witness, so eliminating the propositional component yields an executable artifact whose behavioral specification is enforced by the dependent typechecker at compile time. The conjecture is that any abstract specification expressible in a dependently-typed host language can be compiled into a dependent-intersection refinement whose first component is an extraction target and whose second is a proof of contract adherence, such that projection of the first component is guaranteed to satisfy the second."}
